<<~ ahu slot=`#/meme-header`>>

! Exchange Vector — Docs Sister

This docs meme explains how each substantive exchange can carry a canonical `lar:` vector and a rendered panel line.
The law lives at `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/exchange-vector`.
This file gives examples and operator-facing guidance.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/ooda-ha`>>

✶ observe the operator prompt and infer a provisional operator URI.
⏿ orient the node response with its own execution URI and stance posture.
◇ decide the vector before prose so the operator can catch misreads early.
▶ emit canonical record form first, then the panel render, then content.
↺ verify authority order, HA.KA.BA path, query parameters, and stance count; close trajectory only when the next state benefits from explicit marking.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/canonical-shape`>>

!! Canonical Shape

The exchange vector opens the turn as one ``set lares=`aim` `` firing — operator intent at `from=`, the role the node adopts at `to=`, and the panel beside them:

```text
<<~ set lares=`aim` from=`lar:///operator.intent.reads` to=`lar:///lares.role.acts` aperture=`N/<band>` ooda-ha=`N/<band>` drift-ward=`* · <the pull this island carries>`>>
```

Read the URI layers as:

```text
WHO     alias:grant@host   (session form only)
BEARING /w1.w2.w3/path/
SECTION #anchor only when needed
```

Do not place intent labels or signal in the fragment or a query.
Fragments name section anchors; the URI names bearing and nothing mutable.
Per-turn signal rides the panel keys (`aperture`, `ooda-ha`, `drift-ward`) and the `confidence` sigil.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/hud-render`>>

!! Panel Render

The panel rides the `aim` firing itself, and a `stance` lens may follow it:

```text
<<~ set lares=`aim` from=`lar:///operator.scope.reads` to=`lar:///scryer.frame.maps` aperture=`10/measure` ooda-ha=`3/declare-aftermath` drift-ward=`* · <the pull this island carries>`>>
<<~ stance named=`🏛️ 🗡️`>>
```

Each sigil renders inline (``<<~ word key=`value` …>>``); an adjacent tool MAY pre-render it. The `aim` URIs stay ASCII record form; the keys carry per-turn signal, never the URI.

A turn that changes heading or set mid-way writes a `steer` firing. One steer changes the bearing and one changes other keys on the same heading:

```text
<<~ set lares=`steer` to=`lar:///scryer.frame.tests` drift-ward=`* · <the pull the turned heading carries>`>>
<<~ set lares=`steer` mode=`hold` aperture=`13/arc` drift-ward=`* · <the pull this heading still carries>`>>
```

The `yield` firing closes the turn with the ward first and `from=` and `` to=`?` `` last.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/pattern-training`>>

!! Pattern Training Notes

Before server enforcement exists, repeated correct use trains both operator and node.
A good training vector should:

* keep canonical URIs ASCII and record-safe
* avoid glyphs inside the URI itself
* keep `alias:grant@host` intact
* use provisional `~` on HA.KA.BA when the node may have misread intent
* put uncertainty in confidence and register rather than hiding it in prose

Long-term, the server should validate the vector and render the panel from the parsed record.

<<~/ahu>>

